Manage bookmarks with a menu program and a basic file.

The file is Tab Separated Values, one bookmark per line:

    URL<tab>description<tab>tag tag tag

For example:

    https://equwal.com	Spenser Truex's website.	users

Lines starting with # are ignored. Interact with the file using the bm
script, or any other tool: cut, grep, awk and your editor all work.

== Requirements ==
dmenu (or fzf), one of xclip, xsel or wl-clipboard, a POSIX sh, awk, make.
Optional: curl (page titles, --check), jq (--import of Chromium files), git.

== Install ==
    make install

installs bm to /usr/local/bin. To install somewhere you own instead:

    make PREFIX="$HOME/.local" install

== Setup ==
Nothing is required. Bookmarks are kept in ~/.local/share/sbm/bookmarks
($XDG_DATA_HOME is honoured) and the file is created on first use.

Tag choices are read from ~/.local/share/sbm/usertags. The file syntax is:

    <tag><space>|<space><description><newline>

Start from the included example, or from nothing: a tag typed into the tag
menu that is not in the file yet is added to it.

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/sbm
    cp usertags ~/.local/share/sbm/

To keep the files somewhere else, export their locations from your shell's
startup file. Use $HOME here: a ~ inside quotes is not expanded.

    export BOOKMARKS="$HOME/notes/bookmarks"
    export USERTAGS="$HOME/notes/usertags"

== Usage ==
    bm                   dmenu: prompt for an action. fzf: browse (see below)
    bm -c, --copy        copy a bookmark's URL to the clipboard
    bm -o, --open        open a bookmark in the default browser
                         (-p, --plumb is the same)
    bm -a, --add [url]   add a bookmark
    bm -e, --edit        open the bookmark file in $VISUAL/$EDITOR at a bookmark
    bm -d, --delete      delete a bookmark
    bm -l, --list        print the bookmarks
    bm -s, --search ERE  print the bookmarks matching an extended regex
    bm --import SRC      import bookmarks from a browser (see below)
    bm --check           report dead and permanently moved links
    bm --migrate         convert a file from the old format (see below)
    bm -h, --help

    -t, --tag TAG        restrict any of the above to one tag
    -S, --sort MODE      used (default), recent, url, desc or tag
    --print              with --copy or --open, print the URL instead

== The menu ==
With a display bm uses dmenu, as it always has; fzf is only used when dmenu
is missing or there is no X11 or Wayland display. SBM_MENU=dmenu or
SBM_MENU=fzf settles it either way.

With dmenu, tags are chosen one at a time until "done" is picked or the menu
is closed. With fzf they are chosen all at once: TAB marks a tag, ENTER
confirms. In both, typing a tag that does not exist creates it.

With fzf, plain "bm" is one screen for everything:

    ENTER   open            ctrl-a  add            ctrl-t  filter by tag
    ctrl-y  copy            ctrl-d  delete (asks)  ctrl-s  next sort order
    ESC     leave           ctrl-e  edit

== Adding ==
Without a url, bm offers one: the X11 primary selection, or the clipboard,
when it holds a URL.

The description defaults to the page's <title>, fetched with curl (3 second limit; SBM_FETCH=0 turns this off). With fzf,
ENTER accepts the default. With dmenu the default is the one entry in the
menu: Return accepts it, and Shift-Return sends exactly what you typed.

Adding refuses a URL that is already bookmarked. URLs are compared without
their scheme, a leading www. and trailing slashes, so http://www.example.com/
and https://example.com are the same bookmark.

== Sorting ==
    used     most opened or copied first, then by url (the default)
    recent   most recently added first
    url, desc, tag

Set a default with SBM_SORT. Use counts live in "$BOOKMARKS.usage", so the
bookmark file itself stays a plain list.

== Importing ==
    bm --import bookmarks.html      # the HTML export of any browser
    bm --import vivaldi             # or chrome, brave, edge, chromium
    bm --import path/to/Bookmarks   # a Chromium JSON file (needs jq)

Folder names become tags ("Dev Tools" becomes dev-tools), bookmarks you
already have are skipped, and anything that is not a web, ftp or file URL is
dropped. Importing twice adds nothing.

== History and sync with git ==
If the directory holding the bookmark file is itself a git repository, every
add, delete, edit, import and migrate is committed. bm never pushes or pulls.

    cd ~/.local/share/sbm && git init
    printf '%s\n' '*.usage' '*.bak' > .gitignore

A bookmark file that merely sits somewhere inside a bigger repository (your
dotfiles, say) is left alone unless SBM_GIT=1. SBM_GIT=0 turns commits off.

== Configuration ==
All through the environment, all optional.

    BOOKMARKS     bookmark file
    USERTAGS      tag choices file
    DMENULINES    lines shown by dmenu (default 15)
    SBM_MENU      dmenu, fzf, or a custom command
    SBM_FZF_OPTS  extra options for every fzf call
    SBM_SORT      default sort order
    SBM_COPY      command that reads the new clipboard contents from stdin
    SBM_PASTE     command that writes the clipboard contents to stdout
    SBM_OPEN      command that opens the URL given as its argument
    SBM_FETCH     0 to never download a page for its title
    SBM_GIT       0 never commit, 1 commit even inside a bigger repository

The clipboard is the first of wl-copy, xclip and xsel that exists, and URLs
are opened with xdg-open.

A custom SBM_MENU is called with the choices on stdin as one of:

    <command> pick  <prompt>     print the chosen line
    <command> multi <prompt>     print every chosen line
    <command> ask   <prompt>     print free text; stdin holds a default, if any

Printing nothing means the user cancelled. test/fakemenu is a small example.

== Migrating from the old format ==
Earlier versions wrote "URL description | tag tag". Convert such a file with

    bm-migrate                  # $BOOKMARKS, or the default file
    bm-migrate path/to/file     # any other file
    bm-migrate -n file          # only show what it would become
    bm-migrate - < old > new    # as a filter

bm-migrate is a standalone script: it needs sh and awk, not bm. "bm --migrate"
does the same to the file bm is using.

The URL is the first word of a line, the tags are what follows the last
" | ", and a line without one has no tags. Comments, blank lines and lines
that are already tab separated are left alone, so converting twice changes
nothing. The original is kept next to the file as .bak; an existing .bak is
never overwritten (.bak.1, .bak.2 and so on are used instead).

== Tests ==
    make check

runs shellcheck, when installed, and test/run.sh. The tests script dmenu,
fzf and curl, so they need no display and no network.

